High Miscibility-Induced Reduction of Trap Density in All-Polymer Solar Cells Using Hybrid Cyclohexyl-Hexyl Side Chains
Reducing the trap density within organic solar cells is of vital importance to realize high power conversion efficiency (PCE); however, research focusing on this aspect is limited in all-polymer solar cells (All-PSCs). In this work, it is found that the trap density can be dramatically reduced by simultaneously obtaining high miscibility of donor and acceptor and ordered packing in blend films through substituting ethylhexyl with hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chains in the design of the polymer donor. D18-ChCl with hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl chains has a slightly lower aggregation behavior relative to the D18-Cl counterpart, but reveals synchronously higher miscibility and crystallinity in a blend with the acceptor PYF-T-o. Such a morphology evolution positively affects the electronic properties of the device—prolongs the carrier lifetime, facilitates exciton dissociation, and lowers the energy disorder. As a result, the All-PSC devices based on D18-ChCl exhibited a remarkable PCE of \(17.1\%\), with a low trap density of \(2.65 \times 10^{15} \, \text{cm}^{-3}\), a low energy disorder of 47 meV as well as outstanding stability and mechanical durability. This result demonstrates that hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl alkyl engineering delicately improves miscibility, drives low trap density, and refines device performance, which brings vibrancy to the All-PSC research field.
## 1. Introduction
Solution-processed all polymer solar cells (All-PSCs) continue to be a promising renewable energy technology that possesses the salient advantages of being light-weight and having structure tunability, mechanical flexibility, stability, and being amenable to roll-to-roll manufacturing of large-area devices.[1–6] The active layer, the core of All-PSCs, consisting of a bulk heterojunction (BHJ) of a polymer donor (D) and a polymer acceptor (A), forms a complex film morphology with the goal to synchronously optimizing optoelectronic properties, in particular, absorption and exciton formation, exciton dissociation and charge-carrier transport properties.[7–16] Specifically, the D and A should not only have a certain miscibility to provide sufficient interfaces for exciton dissociation, but also own suitable crystallinity and phase purity and further construct a bicontinuous interpenetrating network
structure to improve the hole and electron transport.[9,11,17–32] However, bicontinuous interpenetrating networks with pure individual donor and acceptor phases are difficult to obtain from semicrystalline polymer blends with distinct disorder, which throws the morphological control work into a dilemma in All-PSCs. Moreover, large trap states and localized electronic states in the forbidden gap of semiconductors can be induced by relatively poor blend morphology, acting as recombination centers, lower the mobility, disturb the internal field distribution, and reduce device performance.[33–42] Therefore, it is very challenging to suppress trap density in the active layer by morphology control for boosting the PCE of All-PSCs.
Trap states are ubiquitous within solar cells ranging from material defects to chemical impurities, from device interfaces to environmental effects, with many of these effects being coupled.[40,43–47] Among them, the main source of trap states is disorder, which includes dynamic and static disorders.[41] The active layer is dynamically disordered due to the thermal mobility of molecules because the condensed-state intermolecular interactions in organic semiconductors are typically weak and heavily
reliant on van der Waals forces.[48] In addition, the static disorder originates from structural defects and inferior morphology of blend films,[49–52] which would bring about high trap density \((10^{16}{-}10^{18}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) .[40,41] At present, there are very few reports on reducing trap density of organic photovoltaic materials for improving the performance of solar cells. For instance, Lin et al. improved the molecular packing order and domain purity of D18: Y6-based BHJ layers by adopting BTPR as third component, reducing the trap density of solar cells from \(2\,\times\,10^{16}\,\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) to \({\approx}8\,\times\,10^{15}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) .[43] Zhao et al. designed TTPIC-4F by extending the \(\pi\) -conjugation of the donor core in an A-D-A type acceptor to achieve strong electronic coupling, strengthened the crystallinity in the BHJ layers, and realized a low trap density of \(4\times10^{15}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) .[53] Even though highly ordered molecular packing and phase purity play a significant role in reducing the trap density of solar cells, the always inevitably enlarged domain size and evidently low miscibility in BHJ layers could throw the morphological control and charge separation into a dilemma, and thus in turn result in relatively low PCE. Hence, paving an avenue to simultaneously obtain small domain size and enhanced miscibility without sacrificing the ordered molecular packing, and to reduce the trap density, may be the key to further improve the performance of All-PSCs.
Cyclohexyl-hexyl side chain engineering, that incorporation of rigid cyclohexane at the tail of hexyl chain in the construction of organic semiconductor materials, is an effective way to tune the molecular stacking and morphology of active layer. This composite side chain has been shown to slightly enlarge the steric hindrance effect and weaken the aggregation of molecular chains, which is expected to further control the intermixing between polymer donor and acceptor.[54–56] Furthermore, cyclohexyl-hexyl chain has also been applied to the design of small molecular donor, which can facilitate suitable nanophase separation and ordered molecular stacking to improve device performance.[28] However, cyclohexyl-hexyl chain engineering has never been used in All-PSCs. Therefore, a fundamental understanding of specific features brought by cyclohexyl-hexyl chain engineering in terms of morphological structure, charge dynamic behavior, and trap density is highly desired, which could bring vibrancy to the All-PSC research field.
In this contribution, we show that the trap density can be strongly reduced by the strategy of obtaining simultaneously good miscibility and ordered packing through cycloalkylalkyl chain engineering in the construction of polymer donors (Figure 1b). As an analog of D18-Cl, the new polymer donor D18- ChCl was designed by substituting the 2-ethylhexyl side chains at the benzodithiophene core with cyclohexyl-hexyl chains, which can provide a further tuning in solubility and molecular stacking. Using PYF-T-o as the acceptor, the D18-ChCl blend realized an interpenetrating network constituting of smaller phase separation, and higher miscibility and crystallinity simultaneously relative to D18-Cl-based blend. Such a morphology evolution positively affects the electronic properties of the device—prolongs the carrier lifetime, facilitates exciton dissociation and charge transport, and lowers the energy disorder, which ultimately boosts the PCE from \(12.3\%\) to \(17.1\%\) , and lowers the trap density from \(4.15\times10^{15}\) to \(2.65\times10^{15}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) . Meanwhile, D18-ChCl-based AllPSCs are found to exhibit excellent stability and bending durability in flexible devices, which maintained \(84\%\) of initial efficiency
after 1500-time bending cycles. What's more, investigations of additional systems highlight the great potential of this hybrid alkyl engineering for facilitating high miscibility of donor and acceptor and driving the reduction of the trap density. Overall, delicately improving the miscibility between donor and acceptor while strongly increasing crystallinity via hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chain engineering is shown to be an effective method to yield low trap density, and high-performance All-PSCs with good efficiency and mechanical toughness.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The chemical structures of D18-Cl, D18-ChCl, and PYF-T-o are displayed in Figure 1a, while the detailed synthetic and characterization procedures are provided in Scheme S1, Supporting Information. The D18-Cl and D18-ChCl polymers are typical D-A conjugated donor polymers prepared via Stille-coupling polymerization \((M_{\mathrm{n}}\,{=}\,77.0\,\mathrm{kDa}\) , \(\mathrm{PDI}\,{=}\,2.27\) for D18-Cl and \(M_{\mathrm{n}}\,{=}\,84.2\,\mathrm{kDa}\) , \(\mathrm{PDI}\,=\,1.96\) for D18-ChCl). An interesting feature, which was observed is that D18-ChCl shows better solubility in commonly used processing solvents (chloroform, toluene, or chlorobenzene) than D18-Cl, implying a changed aggregation behavior. In dilute chloroform solution, D18-Cl and D18-ChCl exhibit distinct UV–vis spectral profiles with strong absorption ranging from 450 to \(625\ \mathrm{nm}\) , but similar molar absorption coefficients of \(5.56~\times\) \(10^{4}\) and \(5.38\times10^{4}\ \mathrm{M}^{-1}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , respectively (Figure S3, Supporting Information). A similar trend can be observed in the solid state as thin films of D18-ChCl exhibit a slightly redshifted absorption in comparison with D18-Cl thin films, corresponding to a slightly smaller bandgap of \(1.97\,\mathrm{eV}\) (Figure 1c). In addition, the reduced absorption shoulder intensity from D18-Cl to D18-ChCl implies weak intermolecular interaction and aggregation behavior induced by the hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl chain modification. By cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements (Figure 1e and Figure S4, Supporting Information), the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO)/ lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) levels of D18-Cl, D18-ChCl, and PYF-T-o were determined to be \(-2.79/-5.53,\,-2.83/-5.53,\) and \(-3.76/-5.68\ \mathrm{eV},\) respectively, which have a similar trend with the results cross-checked by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Based on the above results, the acceptor PYF-T-o was chosen to be blended with the newly designed polymer. The resulting blend films can not only provide complementary absorption ranging from 300 to \(900\;\mathrm{nm}\) to capture sufficient solar energy, but also display suitable molecular energy level alignment between donor and acceptor to provide efficient thermodynamic driving force for charge separation.
Subsequently, contact angle tests were performed to explore the difference of the surface tension \((\gamma)\) between donor polymers, and thus to estimate and miscibility of \(\mathrm{D/A}\) pairs. As shown in Figure 3d and Table 1, through analysis of the contact angles of liquid drops of diiodomethane/water, the \(\gamma\) of neat D18-Cl, D18- ChCl and PYF-T-o were calculated to be 35.3, 39.4, and \(41.8\;\mathrm{mN}\) \(\mathbf{m}^{-1}\) , respectively. The interaction parameters \((\chi)\) between \(\mathrm{D/A}\) pair were further attained using the Flory-Huggins mode by the equation \(\chi=(\sqrt{\gamma_{\mathrm{donor}}-\sqrt{\gamma_{\mathrm{acceptor}}}})^{2}\) . It is evident that the lower disparity in \(\gamma\) is between the \(\mathrm{D/A}\) pair, the higher miscibility will be. The \(\chi_{\mathrm{D18-ChCl}}\) : PYF-T-o and \(\chi_{\mathrm{D18-Cl}}\) : PYF-T-o parameters were determined to be 0.035 and 0.274, respectively. Apparently, the smaller
\(\chi_{\mathrm{D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o}}\) means relatively higher miscibility between D18- ChCl and PYF-T-o, which could hinder the excessive phase separation, promote the formation of interpenetrating networks and facilitate exciton dissociation. These results indicate that hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl chain engineering is an effective strategy to manipulate the surface tension and finally the miscibility of blend films.
To further decipher the variation of the morphology of the blend films induced by the cyclohexyl-hexyl side chain modification, transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and atomic force microscopy (AFM) were carried out. In the TEM image (Figure S6, Supporting Information) of the D18-Cl/PYF-T-o blend, the light domains appeared essentially inhomogeneous and considerably larger, which can be attributed to a low miscibility of
this D/A pair. In stark contrast, the alternately smaller dark and light regions were intuitively observed in the D18-ChCl/PYFT-o blend, implying that the cyclohexyl-hexyl chain modification could promote a morphology evolution with smaller domain sizes. Moreover, in the AFM phase images (Figure 2a), in particular, the D18-ChCl blend shows a distinct nanofibrillar texture with root-mean-square roughness (RMS) of \(1.58\,\mathrm{nm}\), coarser than that of D18-Cl blend \((0.94\,\mathrm{nm})\), implying a higher crystallinity.
To gain insights into the effect of the hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl chain engineering on the molecular packing and crystalline features of the polymer donors and blend films, grazing incidence wide angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) measurement were carried out. The 2D GIWAXS profiles and their corresponding linecuts of in-plane (IP) and out-of-plane (OOP) direction are provided in Figure 2b. It is evident that both D18-Cl and D18-ChCl neat films show a predominant face-on molecular orientation and strong (010) \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking diffraction peaks at \({\approx}1.64\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) in the out-of-plane (OOP) direction, respectively, corresponding to the similar \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking distance of \(3.83\mathrm{~\AA~}\). Besides, D18-ChCl exhibits slightly weaker diffraction intensity than that of D18-Cl, indicating the reduced aggregation behavior, which keep in line with UV–vis measurement. In addition, GIWAXS studies were performed for D18-Cl: PYF-T-o and D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o blend films to investigate the phase and packing behavior. Similar to the neat donor films, both blend films sustained a face-on orientation relative to the substrate, whereas the \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking diffraction peaks shifted to \({\approx}1.63\mathrm{~\AA^{-1}}\) because of their merging with the \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking diffraction peak of PYF-T-o (Figure S7, Supporting Information). As for in-plane (IP) direction, the (100) laminar diffraction peak of D18-Cl: PYF-T-o and D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o were recorded at \(q_{z}\) of 0.27 and \(0.30\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\), corresponding to lamellar stacking distances of 23.3 and \(21.7\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\), respectively, which suggested that the replacement of the ethylhexyl chains with hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chains can shorten the interchain distance and promote better packing ability.[54,55] In addition, according to Scherrer's equation, the respective crystal coherence lengths for the (100) and (010) peaks were calculated to be 62.8 and \(20.9\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) for D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o, slightly smaller than those for D18-Cl: PYF-T-o (70.7 and \(22.6\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\)). Despite the similarity of the CCL parameters, D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o films exhibit a higher degree of crystallinity than the D18-Cl: PYF-T-o samples, as revealed by integrating the area of the (100) and (010) peaks (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The grazing-incidence small-angle X-ray scattering (GISAXS) measurements were used to further investigate the phase separation within these two blend systems by using the Debye–Anderson–Brumberger model (Figure 2c). The D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o blend film has a slightly smaller average domain size \((2R_{\mathrm{g}}=15.1\ \mathrm{nm})\) compared with the D18-Cl: PYF-T-o films \((2R_{\mathrm{g}}=17.2\,\mathrm{nm})\), which further supports the previous results. To make an intuitive illustration about the influence of the side chains on the morphology evolution of D18-Cl- and
