Within the energy depth of \( 0.19{-}0.24\;\mathrm{~eV} \), the D18-Cl: PYFT-o receives a much higher trap density of \( (0.5{-}1.7)\,\times\,10^{16} \) \( \mathrm{cm}^{-3} \), compared to D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o with a trap density of \( (1.8{-}5.2)\times10^{15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3} \) in corresponding region, which coincide well with the previous SCLC results. In addition, the hole and electron mobilities of the D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o-based devices were calculated to be \( 6.12\times10^{-4} \) and \( 4.15\,\times\,10^{-4}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\,\mathrm{s}^{-1} \), respectively, and are distinctly higher as for the D18-Cl: PYF-T-o-based devices \( (1.32\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1} \) for hole mobility and 2.28 \( \times\,10^{-4}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1} \) for electron mobility) (Figure S10, Supporting Information). The elevated charge-carrier mobilities echoes the conspicuously reduced trap density, implying that D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o has greater potential in All-PSCs than D18-Cl: PYF-T-o.
In view of the disparity in trap density for D18-Cl: PYF-T-o and D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o blend, we further fabricated All-PSCs with an architecture of ITO (indium tin oxide)/PEDOT:PSS (poly[3,4- ethylenedioxythiophene]: poly[styrenesulfonate])/polymer don
or: PYF-T-o/PDINN/Ag to evaluate their photovoltaic performance. The detailed device preparation process and optimized conditions are provided in the Supporting Information. Figure 4a displays the \( J{-}V \) curves with the corresponding photovoltaic parameters collected in Table 2. Compared to D18-Cl: PYFT-o, the \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) (0.905 V) values of the D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o-based devices are almost unchanged, whereas the \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) increases from 22.97 to \( 25.72\ \mathrm{mA\cm}^{-2} \) and FF increases from \( 59.5\% \) to \( 73.4\% \); accordingly, the PCE dramatically improves from \( 12.3\% \) to \( 17.1\% \). Moreover, to the best of our knowledge, the PCE of \( 17.1\% \) is one of highest value for All-PSCs so far, also supporting the potential of hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chains in polymer donors. External quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of optimal devices are presented in Figure 4b. The D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o devices display remarkably stronger photon response in the whole region with the maximum value approaching \( 88\% \) at \( 600\,\mathrm{nm} \), compared with D18-Cl: PYF-T-o device. The integrated \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) from the EQE spectra are 22.39 and \( 24.52\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2} \) for D18-Cl: PYF-T-o and D18-ChCl:
PYF-T-o device, respectively, which match well \( (\approx\!4\% \) mismatch) with the values obtained from the \( J{-}V \) curves.[62]
To decipher the disparity in photovoltaic performance for D18- Cl: PYF-T-o and D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o devices, the exciton dissociation was first probed by plotting the photocurrent density \( (J_{\mathrm{ph}}) \) as a function of the effective voltage \( (V_{\mathrm{eff}}) \) (Figure 4c).[63] With the increasing \( V_{\mathrm{eff}} \) to \( 4\,\mathrm{V} \), \( J_{\mathrm{ph}} \) approaches saturation photocurrent densities \( (J_{\mathrm{sat}}) \). Thus, the exciton dissociation probabilities \( (P_{\mathrm{diss}}=J_{\mathrm{sc}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}) \) under short-circuit conditions for D18-Cl- and D18-ChCl- based devices were determined to be \( 93.2\% \) and \( 97.2\% \), respectively. Moreover, charge collection efficiencies \( (P_{\mathrm{coll}}=J_{\mathrm{MPP}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}} \), \( J_{\mathrm{MPP}} \) is the current at the maximum power point of \( 68.7\% \) and \( 83.4\% \) were revealed for the D18-Cl- and D18-ChCl-based devices, respectively. The higher \( P_{\mathrm{diss}} \) and \( P_{\mathrm{coll}} \) values obtained in D18- ChCl: PYF-T-o-based All-PSCs indicate superior exciton dissociation as well as the better charge collection, which could account for to the higher \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) and FF values in corresponding devices. We speculate that the device with low trap density is beneficial to the diffusion of excitons to the D/A interface, so as to obtain high dissociation efficiency in D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o devices. Furthermore, the higher crystallinity and charge carrier mobilities possibly lead to a better charge collection efficiency value.[64] The degree of bimolecular recombination and geminate recombination was also estimated by the dependence of current density and voltage on light intensity \( (P_{\mathrm{light}}) \), which can be described as \( J_{\mathrm{sc}}\propto P_{\mathrm{light}}^{\mathrm{~\,~\,~}\alpha} \) and \( V_{\mathrm{oc}}\propto n k T/q \) ln \( P_{\mathrm{light}}^{\mathrm{~\,~\,~}} \), respectively. As shown in Figure 4d, the factor \( \alpha \) and \( \mathbf{n} \) for D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o devices are found to be 0.96 and 1.16, respectively, which are closer than 1 than the corresponding values for D18-Cl: PYF-T-o devices (0.95
and 1.20), indicative of the considerably suppressed bimolecular and geminated recombination, and efficient free charge collection in the former. The weaker bimolecular/geminate recombination could be associated with higher charge-carrier mobilities in D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o devices due to their lower trap density.
To investigate the effects of hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chain engineering on the mechanical properties of the blend films, detailed mechanical characterizations were carried out via
different methods. The film-on-elastomer (FOE) method was used to study the deformation and crack formation of blend films during slowly stretching condition.[65,66] The optical microscopy images of blend films were depicted in Figure S12, Supporting Information. From the images obtained during elongation, the D18-Cl: PYF-T-o blend exhibited brittle cracking at \(9.1\%\) strain, whereas larger crack onset strain of \(10.5\%\) can be observed for D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o blend. This indicates that D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o film features better flexibility and ductility properties in comparison with D18-Cl: PYF-T-o film. Next, we analyze the intrinsic tensile properties of thin film via pseudo free-standing tensile testing method, in which films unaffected by the substrate are floated on the water surface.[65,67] The obtained stress–strain curves showed that the D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o film exhibits a slightly improved mechanical ductility, with the fracture strain and elastic moduli of \(5.6\%/535\,\mathrm{\:MPa}\) compared to the D18-Cl: PYF-T-o blend \((5.0\%/602\ \mathrm{MPa})\) (Figure 5b,c). The higher crack onset strain of blend film can be attributed to the high miscibility between polymer donor and polymer acceptor domains induced by hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chain engineering that form intense chain entanglement, which improves the dissipation of external stresses. This result further verified the enhanced flexibility and tensile properties of the D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o film, which are consistent with FOE measurements. Therefore, substituting ethylhexyl with hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chains could a potential strategy to improve the stretchability and reduce the stiffness of the blend film.
Taking advantage of mechanical stretchability of all polymer blend films,[68,69] flexible devices were also fabricated with architecture of AgNW/PEDOT: PSS/active layer/PDINN/Ag (Figure 5a). The current \(J{-}V\) curves under optimized condition and corresponding photovoltaic parameters are presented in Figure 5d and Table S4, Supporting Information. The D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o blend showed a high PCE of \(14.7\%\) in the flexible device, exceeding that of D18-Cl: PYF-T-o-based device \((\mathrm{PCE}=9.7\%)\). The mechanical stability of two devices was further assessed by repeat bending experiment (Figure S13, Supporting Information). After consecutively bending and relaxing up to 1500 times with a certain curvature radius of \(4\,\mathrm{mm}\), \(79.8\%\) and \(84.4\%\) of the initial PCEs were retained for D18-Cl: PYF-T-o- and D18-ChCl: PYF-T-o-based devices, manifesting the better tolerance and mechanical robustness of the latter (Figure 5e).
The outstanding stability of All-PSCs represents an important merit for their potential application in the near future. To evaluate the long-term stability of two devices, we first measured device performances under nitrogen atmosphere (Figure 5f and Figure S14, Supporting Information). The D18-ChCl-based devices showed excellent shelf-life stability, maintaining \(91.7\%\) of its initial efficiencies after \(1000\,\mathrm{~h~}\), while the performance of D18-Cl-based devices dropped by more than \(16\%\) over the same time period. The thermal stability of the two devices was also tested (Figure 5f), where the D18-ChCl-based device exhibited a steady performance with a loss of \(25\%\) after \(100\,\mathrm{~h~}\) of thermal aging at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In contrast, the D18-Cl-based devices displayed
dramatically faster degradation, retaining only \(50\%\) of their initial efficiencies under similar test conditions. Encouragingly, D18-ChCl-based devices showed better stability than the D18-Cl system, probably attributed to the high \(\mathrm{D/A}\) miscibility and ordered packing of blend films with low trap density that can avoid phase rearrangement and performance degradation. This result, thus, further confirmed the advantages of the hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl chain engineering in enhancing the intrinsic stabilities of AllPSCs.
To illustrate the broad utility of our cyclohexyl-hexyl side chain engineering to validate that good miscibility favored the reduction of the trap density in All-PSCs, two additional polymer acceptors, PY-DT and PYIT-OD were used to prove the concept. The chemical structures of the polymer acceptors are depicted in Figure 6a. Contact angle measurement showed a consistent trend that better miscibility between D18-ChCl and polymer acceptor PY-DT/PYIT-OD can be observed due to the lower discrepancy in the surface tension between donor and acceptor (Figure 6b, Figure S15 and Table S5, Supporting Information). Next, the calculated average density of trap state via SCLC method for D18-ChCl: PY-DT and D18-ChCl: PYIT-OD devices were \(8.55 \times 10^{15}\) and \(7.47 \times 10^{15} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\), obviously smaller than those of D18-Cl: PY-DT and D18-Cl: PYIT-OD devices (Figure 6c). What's more, by replacing D18-Cl with D18-ChCl, enhanced \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF values were recorded in both PY-DT- and PYIT-OD-based All-PSCs, which yields a promising efficiency of \(14.1\%\) and \(16.4\%\), respectively (Figure 6d and Table S6, Supporting Information). Together, studies of multiple material systems highlight the great potential of cyclohexyl-hexyl side chain engineering for facilitating high miscibility of donor and acceptor and driving the reduction of the trap density, and further realizing high performance All-PSCs.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, we employ a facile strategy to reduce the trap density in high performance All-PSCs, by simultaneously obtaining high miscibility and ordered packing in blend films through substituting the ethylhexyl with a hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chains in the construction of polymer donors. Using PYF-T-o as the acceptor, the D18-ChCl blend realized an interpenetrating network constituting of smaller phase separation, higher miscibility, condensed molecular packing, and better mechanical robustness relative to the D18-Cl blend. We found that such shaped morphology correlates positively with the electronic properties of the device—prolonge the carrier lifetime, facilitates exciton dissociation, and charge transport and lowers the energy disorder, which ultimately boosts the PCE from \(12.3\%\) to \(17.1\%\), and weakens the trap density from \(4.15 \times 10^{15}\) to \(2.65 \times 10^{15} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\). In addition, the D18-ChCl-based flexible All-PSC also achieved a high PCE of \(14.7\%\) and retained \(>84\%\) of its initial PCE after 1500-time bending cycles. Furthermore, the good D/A miscibility and ordered packing of blend films with low trap density can avoid phase rearrangement and performance degradation. Although in this study, the hybrid cyclohexyl-hexyl side chain engineering has been exemplified with D18-Cl-based copolymers, its practicability, especially the miscibility and molecular stacking manipulation can be expanded to other kinds of polymers and may further trigger
the reduction of trap density for high performance All-PSCs and other electronic applications.
